    <!-- Watch Out For -->
    <section class="exam-section" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #fff3cd 0%, #ffe5a0 100%); border-left-color: #ffc107;">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation section-icon" style="color: #856404;"></i> Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Negative Numbers:</strong> Students often confuse "Sign and Magnitude" with "Two's Complement."</li>
            <li><strong>K-Map Headings:</strong> Forgetting that Gray Code (00, 01, 11, 10) is mandatory for headings to ensure only one bit changes at a time.</li>
            <li><strong>XOR Logic:</strong> Mistaking XOR for a standard OR gate (XOR is strictly "one or the other, but not both").</li>
            <li><strong>Interrupts:</strong> Believing an interrupt is an "error" rather than a routine hardware signal for CPU attention.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

